\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:experimental_setup}

We evaluate how prompt schema size and inter-type confusability affect zero-shot LLM extraction across three information extraction tasks and four datasets: named entity recognition (NER), sentence-level relation extraction (RE), document-level RE, and event detection (ED).

\subsection{Tasks and Datasets}
\label{sec:datasets}

\paragraph{Few-NERD (NER).}
Few-NERD \citep{fewnerd-2021} provides 66 fine-grained entity types organized into 8 coarse categories (person, location, organization, building, art, product, event, other). The dataset spans diverse domains and is suitable for cross-domain evaluation. We sample 1{,}000 instances uniformly from the test set; type frequency follows the natural long-tail distribution of Few-NERD. Evaluation uses token-level text matching. Pairwise analysis covers 1{,}953 type pairs with non-zero co-occurrence out of $\binom{66}{2}=2{,}145$ possible pairs.

\paragraph{FewRel (RE).}
FewRel \citep{fewrel-2018} contains 80 relation types sourced from Wikidata. Since the validation split covers only 16 relations, we combine the training and validation splits to obtain the full 80-relation pool required for schema-scale experiments, and sample 1{,}000 balanced instances (12--13 per relation type, stratified to ensure equal representation). A held-out validation confirms SCS--confusion generalization across the split boundary (Appendix~\ref{app:scs-heldout-validation}). Each instance provides a sentence with marked head and tail entities; the task is to classify the relation. FewRel's balanced design (700 instances per relation type) eliminates type frequency as a potential confound; this balance makes it the primary dataset for BCS analysis. Contamination analysis (Appendix~\ref{app:split-origin}) shows extraction difficulty correlates inversely with training-set prevalence, and BCS effects are unchanged when controlling for contamination.

\paragraph{DocRED (Document-level RE).}
DocRED \citep{docred-2019} contains 96 relation types sourced from Wikidata, evaluated at the document level where entity pairs may span multiple sentences. We sample 1{,}000 instances balanced across 96 relation types ($\sim$10 per type) from the DocRED development set and formulate the task identically to FewRel: given a document with marked head and tail entities, classify the relation. DocRED extends the RE evaluation to a larger schema (96 vs.\ 80 types) and document-level context, testing whether confusability effects persist under longer input contexts and cross-sentence reasoning.

\paragraph{MAVEN (ED).}
MAVEN \citep{maven-2020} defines 168 event types derived from the FrameNet ontology; all types belong to a single ontology (FrameNet), so this is a same-domain schema. We use the validation set (8{,}042 sentences containing 18{,}904 event mentions) for evaluation, as test set labels are not publicly available. The task requires both trigger identification and event type classification. MAVEN serves a supplementary role because its same-domain schema produces centrality confounds at the batch level (\S\ref{sec:centrality_confound}).

Table~\ref{tab:datasets} summarizes the dataset statistics, including the Schema Confusability Score (SCS) ranges defined in \S\ref{sec:scs}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llrrlll}
\toprule
\textbf{Dataset} & \textbf{Task} & \textbf{\# Types} & \textbf{\# Eval Units} & \textbf{Domain} & \textbf{BCS Range} & \textbf{Embedding} \\
\midrule
Few-NERD & NER & 66 & 1{,}000 & Cross & 0.77--0.93 & Qwen3-32B \\
FewRel & RE (sent.) & 80 & 1{,}000 & Cross & 0.42--0.97 & MiniLM \\
DocRED & RE (doc.) & 96 & 1{,}000 & Cross & 0.53--0.73 & MiniLM \\
MAVEN & ED & 168 & 18{,}904\rlap{$^\dagger$} & Same & 0.87--0.97 & MiniLM \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Dataset statistics. BCS Range: min--max over count-curve batches. Embedding: model used for SCS/BCS; ranges are not comparable across embedding models. $^\dagger$Trigger mentions across 8{,}042 sentences.}
\label{tab:datasets}
\end{table}

\subsection{Schema Confusability Score (SCS)}
\label{sec:scs}

\looseness=-1
We define the Schema Confusability Score (SCS) to quantify semantic overlap between types in a schema. Given a type $t$ with name string $\text{name}(t)$, an embedding function $\text{embed}(\cdot)$ maps the type name to a vector representation. The \textbf{pairwise SCS} between types $t_i$ and $t_j$ is:
{\small
\[
\text{SCS}(t_i, t_j) = \cos\bigl(\text{embed}(\text{name}(t_i)),\; \text{embed}(\text{name}(t_j))\bigr)
\]
}

For a batch of types $B = \{t_1, \ldots, t_k\}$, the \textbf{Batch Confusability Score (BCS)} aggregates all pairwise similarities:
\[
\text{BCS}(B) = \frac{1}{\binom{k}{2}} \sum_{i < j,\; t_i, t_j \in B} \text{SCS}(t_i, t_j)
\]

We use two embeddings to provide complementary perspectives: Qwen3 captures the extraction model's own type representations (self-similarity), while MiniLM provides an independent general-purpose baseline. Cross-embedding consistency holds on all datasets (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_bcs_sensitivity}); MiniLM consistently yields equal or stronger correlations, which confirms that Qwen3-based estimates are conservative. We compute SCS with: (1)~Qwen3-32B \citep{qwen3-2025} hidden-state representations (5{,}120-d) and (2)~all-MiniLM-L6-v2 \citep{minilm-2020,sentence-bert-2019}, a 384-d sentence encoder. Few-NERD uses Qwen3 because its fine-grained same-category pairs (e.g., ``person-artist'' vs.\ ``person-actor'') require the higher-dimensional space. MiniLM is the primary BCS embedding because sentence-transformers are trained for semantic similarity \citep{sentence-bert-2019}; Qwen3 causal-LM states show compressed BCS range (std${}\,=\,0.034$ vs.\ MiniLM std${}\,=\,0.081$), consistent with autoregressive anisotropy \citep{ethayarajh-contextual-2019}. Name-only SCS yields equivalent predictive accuracy to name+definition ($\Delta r$: $-0.023$ to $+0.035$; Appendix~\ref{app:scs-ablation}).

\paragraph{SCS as a lightweight proxy.}
\label{sec:scs-validation}
SCS is a pre-hoc proxy computable without running extraction. Pairwise SCS correlates with observed type confusion (Few-NERD $r = 0.112$, FewRel $r = 0.187$, both $p < 0.001$; Table~\ref{tab:pairwise}), though context, frequency, and definition quality also contribute (\S\ref{sec:pairwise}). SCS does not capture context-dependent disambiguation or mention-level ambiguity; these factors contribute to the residual variance in pairwise analysis.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.48\textwidth}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{../../figures/paper/fig_count_curves.pdf}
\end{minipage}
\hfill
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.48\textwidth}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{../../figures/paper/fig_bcs_scaling.pdf}
\end{minipage}
\caption{\textbf{(a)} Count-degradation curves. Error bars: $\pm 1$\,SD across 3 batch-composition seeds $\times$ 25 batches. RE declines monotonically; NER shows a U-shape. Rightmost points are full-schema (negligible sampling variance). \textbf{(b)} BCS-controlled FewRel accuracy. Slopes are consistent across the independent and within-pool 16-relation conditions ($p = 0.60$); wider BCS range at 80 relations produces larger gaps.}
\label{fig:main_results}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Experimental Design}
\label{sec:exp_design}

We conduct two complementary experimental paradigms.

\paragraph{Experiment 1: Count Curves.}
We vary the number of types presented in the prompt schema to measure performance as a function of schema size. For each count level, we sample 25 random type batches for Few-NERD and FewRel (5 for DocRED and MAVEN)\footnote{DocRED and MAVEN use fewer batches due to higher per-batch evaluation cost (document-level RE and 8{,}042 sentences, respectively).} and evaluate the same 1{,}000 test samples across all batches. At count${}=1$, every type is evaluated individually as a solo baseline. The remaining count levels follow natural partition points in each schema's type hierarchy rather than uniform spacing: \{2, 6, 12, 22, 44, 66\} for Few-NERD, \{4, 8, 16, 32, 40, 64, 80\} for FewRel, \{4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 96\} for DocRED, and \{6, 12, 22, 44, 64\} for MAVEN.

\paragraph{Experiment 2: BCS-Controlled.}
We fix type count and vary BCS to isolate confusability from schema size. For each count level, we construct one low-BCS batch, one high-BCS batch, and 25 random batches (27 total). Extreme batches are constructed via greedy search over the pairwise SCS matrix: the low-BCS batch starts from the least-similar type pair and iteratively adds the type with lowest maximum similarity to already-selected types; the high-BCS batch mirrors this with maximum similarity. We evaluate on Few-NERD (count$\,\in\{6, 12\}$), FewRel (count$\,\in\{8, 16\}$, both 16-relation (pilot) and 80-relation pools), and DocRED (count$\,\in\{16, 32\}$). Within-pool 16-relation subsets from the 80-relation pool isolate confusability from pool composition. MAVEN results are reported separately due to a centrality confound (\S\ref{sec:centrality_confound}).

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}
\label{sec:metrics}

\textbf{NER}: text-match F1, where a prediction is correct if the exact text string matches after lowercasing and whitespace normalization, \emph{and} the type matches; partial spans count as incorrect. \textbf{RE}: accuracy (exact match of predicted relation label to gold). Each batch evaluates only samples whose gold relation is included in the candidate set; out-of-schema samples are excluded before inference. The prompt allows a \texttt{none} response for cases where the model judges no candidate applies; any such prediction is scored as incorrect since the gold is always among the candidates by construction. \textbf{ED}: relaxed F1, where any token overlap between predicted and gold trigger spans, with correct event type.


\subsection{Model and Inference}
\label{sec:model}

\looseness=-1
We use Qwen3-32B-FP8 \citep{qwen3-2025} served via vLLM v0.20.2 \citep{vllm-2023} on a single NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 GPU (96\,GB) with greedy decoding (temperature${}\,=\,0$, seed 42). Two NER/ED prompt versions are evaluated: v1 requests character offsets (evaluated via text matching); v2 adds format constraints that double precision with minimal recall loss. RE uses a single prompt version throughout. Both versions produce consistent degradation patterns; we report both where applicable to quantify formatting sensitivity \citep{sclar-sensitivity-2024} (templates in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_prompts}). Cross-model validation uses Meta-Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct (FP8) and Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct \citep{llama3-2024}. All experiments use single-seed evaluation with greedy decoding, which substantially reduces model-level randomness (residual SD = 0.88\,pp at count 80 under greedy decoding, likely attributable to FP8 quantization non-determinism); bootstrap CIs capture the remaining sampling variance from batch composition. Pair-level CIs use percentile bootstrap ($B{=}10{,}000$); batch-level CIs use $B{=}1{,}000$.

